  "summary": "Architectural approach that emphasizes the visible connection between building elements and their assembly.",
  "description": "Tectonics focuses on expressing how building components come together, celebrating the joints, connections, and transitions between materials and elements. This approach emerged as a reaction against both the concealed construction of modernism and the exaggerated expression of high-tech architecture.",
  "culturalContext": "Tectonics in architecture — from Greek tekton (carpenter) through Semper and Frampton's theorizing — privileges the visible, honest join: the structural poetry of load meeting support, material meeting material, as against scenographic surface. Swiss \"tectonic school\" (Pascal Flammer, Valerio Olgiati), Japanese joinery (Kuma's kizukuri), and the \"construction as expression\" teaching tradition keep it central; critics read tectonic legibility as architecture's ethical baseline.",
